#!/usr/bin/env perl

use 5.020;
use strict;
use warnings;
use Devel::XSCover ();

exit Devel::XSCover->new_from_argv(@ARGV)->run;

__END__

=head1 NAME

xs_cover - XS and C coverage for a distribution in one command

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    xs_cover [options]

    xs_cover                         build with coverage, make test under Devel::Cover,
                                     read the counters, merge into cover_db, run cover
    xs_cover -report html_basic      the report, passed through to cover (XSCover by default)
    xs_cover -gcov /path/to/gcov-14  the gcov tool, when detection picks the wrong one
    xs_cover -test t/05-x.t          run that test file with prove instead of make test
    xs_cover -plain t/05-forks.t     run that file without Devel::Cover
    xs_cover -no_perl                run everything without Devel::Cover, the C side alone
    xs_cover -ignore 'vendor/'       add a pattern to the default ignores
    xs_cover                         with a .xs_cover file in the dist holding such options
    xs_cover -keep                   keep the counters and instrumented objects after the run
    xs_cover -no_delete              keep an existing cover_db instead of starting afresh
    xs_cover -db cover_db            the database directory
    xs_cover -make make              the make command
    xs_cover -cover /path/to/cover   the cover command, when the one beside perl is wrong
    xs_cover -timeout 600            end a test run that passes that many seconds
    xs_cover -dry                    print every command and run nothing
    xs_cover -silent                 print only the summary
    xs_cover -h                      this help
    xs_cover -v                      the version

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Run in a distribution directory, C<xs_cover> rebuilds every XS and C source with
the compiler's coverage instrumentation, runs the test suite under Devel::Cover so
the Perl side is covered as usual, reads the counters the compiler wrote, merges
them into the same coverage database, and runs C<cover> for the report. The exit
status is the test run's.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over

=item -report NAME

The report C<cover> makes, C<XSCover> by default: F<coverage.html> under the database
and the summary table printed. C<text> prints every file annotated.

=item -gcov PATH

The gcov tool to run. By default clang's C<llvm-cov gcov> or gcc's C<gcov> of the
same version as the compiler.

=item -test FILE

Run FILE with C<prove -b -j1> instead of C<make test>. May be given more than once.

=item -plain FILE

Run FILE without Devel::Cover. Run plain, a file's Perl side is not covered and
its C side is. With a plain file the suite runs through C<prove -b -j1> in two
parts, the rest under Devel::Cover and the plain files without it. May be given
more than once. A test file that uses threads is run plain without being named,
since Devel::Cover does not work with threads and the file would fail at compile
time; the run says which.

=item -threads_covered

Leave test files that use threads in the covered run.

=item -no_perl

Run the whole suite without Devel::Cover. The report then has the C side alone. For
a distribution whose module reads or rewrites the op tree, which sees Devel::Cover's
instrumentation and behaves differently under it, or whose Perl side is a line of
C<XSLoader>.

=item -ignore PATTERN

A regular expression on a source path. Matching files are left out of the report.
F<ppport.h> and every file outside the distribution are left out already.

A distribution can keep this and any other option in a F<.xs_cover> file in its
directory, one or more words a line, C<#> starting a comment: the file's words go
before the command line.

=item -keep

Keep the counters, the gcov text (in F<cover_db.gcov>) and the instrumented
objects after the run.
Without it the sources are touched again so the next plain C<make> rebuilds them
optimised.

=item -no_delete

Keep an existing coverage database. By default it is removed first, as C<cover
-test> does.

=item -db DIR

The coverage database, F<cover_db> by default.

=item -make COMMAND

The make command, C<make> by default.

=item -cover COMMAND

The C<cover> command. By default the one installed beside the running perl.

=item -timeout SECONDS

End the test run after that many seconds, ending its process group, and exit
with 124. No limit by default.

=item -dry

Print every command that would run and run none of them.

=item -silent

Print only the summary.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

A whole distribution, from its directory:

    xs_cover

The report is then F<cover_db/coverage.html>, with one page per file beside it, and
the summary table is printed.

One test file, through C<prove>:

    xs_cover -test t/05-parse.t

A suite with a test that forks a server the harness cannot cover: run that one
without Devel::Cover, and its C side is still counted (a test that uses threads is
found and run that way by itself):

    xs_cover -plain t/31-server.t

A module that rewrites the op tree, whose suite behaves differently under
Devel::Cover, with the C side alone:

    xs_cover -no_perl

Several runs into one database, say two suites or two sets of options:

    xs_cover -test t/01-a.t
    xs_cover -no_delete -test t/02-b.t

Keep the counters and the gcov text after the run, for a look by hand:

    xs_cover -keep

The report alone, on a database already written:

    cover -report XSCover

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<Devel::XSCover>, L<Devel::Cover>, L<cover>

=cut
